#[chapter/10/lore]{
  notes: [
    "Bone.land is the small constitution — the spine that holds when the veil shakes. Even its pies keep their joints exposed."
    "Mx. BoneWAP, a pie of clean-boiled honesty, audits karmic momentum: the smallest wishes have the longest arcs."
    "The finding, published: BaneWAP's grand wish would cost a generation of coincidences. BaneWAP stops pretending to wait."
    "Bone.land frames are chalked on the floor in braces and marks before anyone raises them; the mortise marks — ^ where a beam lifts, . where it stands — are what is left of the argument, so..."
    "Every beam is told its load out loud on raising day. Load-beetles in the timber click when a beam is asked to bear more than it agreed to; load-bells under the joists ring wherever weight..."
    "BoneWAP's listening mallet is felt-headed: a beam struck hard says only that it was struck; a beam asked properly says what it will bear."
    "Chimney-swifts carried the frame's note to kitchens that had never heard of Bone.land; the Rift hall's crack-gauge read it as the same law on a younger floor, nothing owed yet."
    "Chalk is cheaper than oak: every joiner says it, and the bench by the door is why — cut from the one beam in living memory raised before it was chalked. It is very comfortable; nobody lik..."
    "Bone.land keeps Council Hill's rule, write it before you argue it, and adds only that you do it on the floor in chalk. Two carters arguing the frame's sum found, halfway across the boards..."
  ]
}

